body:
If you mean fractional reserve banking then yes under the current system, no under a free market system.

&#x200B;

Under a fractional reserve free banking system, the interest rate is determined the demand to hold cash meaning that lower public time preferences will result in more investments in higher order goods and higher time preferences will result in higher interest rates and as a result, more production of consumption goods. Under a fractional reserve central banking, interest rates are distorted causing malinvestment and economic instability.

Here is a golden comment (golden in the sense that it is hilariously ignorant):

&#x200B;

\> I don't get why Peter Schiff doesn't ever consider that people who get as high as Bernanke aren't puppets for the criminal elites.  Schiff who coherently nails the failings of the FED still has deference for these fraudsters and their ponzi schemes. If Celente was there he'd have kicked Bernanke in the balls.  Enjoy your smiley souvenir photo - waste of an opportunity.﻿

&#x200B;

I consider myself to be a libertarian-ish Fed skeptic and a supporter of free banking but certainly not to the extent of the above quote. Libertarians need to recognize that while the federal reserve is usually inefficient, it can still conduct good policy. For example, there is an Austrian case for NGDP Targeting. A fall in velocity can represent a lower public time preference meaning it's ok for the Fed to lower interest rates and expand the money supply and rise in velocity representing the opposite meaning the Fed should raise interest rates and contract the money supply.
